  {
    "episode_id": 49762,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49762.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "Well, Jamie, thank you for that feedback and for using the My Chingo service. I'm glad you liked it, and I'm glad that you liked the book also. If anyone out there is interested, you can pick up tricks of the podcasting masters at Amazon. There's a link on the front page of podcast 4-1. I'll take you right to it. Now, a little background, before we get into the interview here with the senator, and that background is on myself, just to let you know where I'm coming from in the telecommunication industry. In the past, for companies I've worked for, I've had to sell and design into the telecommunication industry. Matter of fact, one company I worked for, I was actually on a committee that rewrote one of the telecom specs. That spec is GR1244 core issue two, and I was on that committee with four other companies, and the other companies were BellSouth, Quest, SBC and Verizon, and then there was myself from the company I work for, which was an electronic manufacturer. Additionally, I was a company's delegate into the OIF, which stands for Optical Internet Working Forum. So a good part of my career was spent either working with, working for, or selling into the telecommunication industry. Yes, it was the bowels of the industry, but it's those bowels that we're now fighting over when it comes to net neutrality. And for a little background on today's guest, Senator Ted Stevens was born November 18, 1923. He is the longest serving Republican in the US Senate, and he is the Chairman of the United States Senate Committee on Commerce, Science and Transportation. Without further ado, let's get into the interview. Here we go. Senator, welcome to the show. Now, traditionally, I would ask you a first question about what was your first computer. But because you're so involved in the whole internet and net neutrality, I think I'd start you out with this question. When is it that you first used the internet yourself?",
