    "turn_text": "it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, it, Think of it like paying a toll to get onto the information superhighway. But all you're allowed to do if you pay the standard toll is get on the access roads. If you actually want to get on the superhighway, you have to pay a separate toll and a much higher toll. And this would stifle competition. This would stifle the small companies that are just starting out. YouTube would never have gotten off the ground. Amazon.com would never have gotten off the ground. Google would never have gotten off the ground. What these companies are trying to do is restrict content that they don't own and control to the access roads where the experience to the user is so bad the user is willing to go to a subpar solution that they provide because it's faster. not because it's better, not because it's more innovative, but because it's faster. And by taking the competitive threats and putting them off on the access roads of the information superhighway, where they have to stop for traffic lights, get caught in traffic jams, and aren't out there doing 75 miles an hour like their data is doing. They're going to force users away from new innovative products where the companies don't have the money to fund to get onto the super highway and are stuck on these access roads. That's what net neutrality is against.",
