  {
    "episode_id": 49762,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49762.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Senator, exactly. That is the point of net neutrality. We don't want discriminatory practices. We don't want a two-tiered system. We want the system that we have today. We don't want there to be a way for the big carriers to manipulate and take over certain sections of the internet for their own use and for only those that they partner with. We want any small company, any innovative company to have the same access to the backbone of the internet that the big companies and any company today has access to. That's the whole point. We don't want discriminatory practices. We want it to be fair, not just for the people that are providing services, but also for the users of those services. We want it fair across the board. Senator, those of us that support net neutrality, we didn't start this whole issue. It was the carriers. It was their actions, their words that started this net neutrality movement. We are not here to be blamed for this movement. We are here to make sure it gets carried out. We want the internet to stay the way it's been up until this point in time. But things are changing. The carriers are saying things, the carriers are doing things that are causing us to push forward with the net neutrality issue.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
