    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Well, Senator, with all due respect, that is completely untrue. There are documented cases. In 2004, North Carolina ISP Madison River blocked their DSL customers from using any rival web-based phone service. In 2005, Canada's telephone giant tell us blocked customers from visiting a website sympathetic to the telecommunications workers union during a contentious labor dispute. Shaw, a major Canadian cable, internet and telephone service company, intentionally downgrades the quality and reliability of competing internet phone services that their customers might choose, driving customers to their own phone service. Not through better services, but by rigging the marketplace. This is what we're worried about. And let's not forget about this little piece that came out of the Washington Post, quote, William L. Smith, Chief Technology Officer for Atlanta-based Bell South, one of the four largest R-Box told reporters and analysts that an Internet service provider, such as his firm, should be able, for example, to charge Yahoo Inc. for the opportunity to have its search site load faster than that of Google Inc. this is what we're against, rigging the system.",
